    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "From polite to plain",
     "texto": [
      "Plain-form verbs are the dictionary forms: **いく** (to go), **たべる** (to eat). Negation ends in **ない**: **いかない** (not to go), **たべない** (not to eat). The past ends in **た**: **いった** (went), **たべた** (ate).",
      "For \"to go\" and \"to eat\" in the polite form, on the other hand, you have **いきます** and **たべます**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Adjectives and the verb \"to be\"",
     "texto": [
      "**です** becomes **だ**: **これ は ほん だ** (this is a book). The negation is **じゃない** (is not), and the past is **だった**.",
      "Adjectives ending in -i barely change: **さむい** (it's cold) → **さむかった** (it was cold)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
